# supply-chain-graph

[![CI](https://github.com/vendraholdings/supply-chain-graph/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/vendraholdings/supply-chain-graph/actions/workflows/ci.yml)

A directed supplier-to-customer graph of <<n_companies>> listed semiconductor companies in eleven tiers, with the source and filing date of every edge,
and tests of how shocks and returns move across the tiers.

> **Status.** The graph holds <<n_pairs>> supplier-customer pairs (<<n_edge_rows>> edge-years) covering <<n_connected>> companies: <<n_curated_pairs>> curated by hand and the rest extracted by rules from <<n_filings>> annual reports of <<n_filers>> SEC filers. The automated edges are confirmed by a small language model or by manual review.
> On these edges, customer momentum earns an annualised net Sharpe ratio of <<sr_net>> and cannot be distinguished from randomly rewired links; the event study finds no significant abnormal return.

![Rotating view of the supplier-to-customer network, coloured by tier](docs/hero.gif)

*Figure 1. The universe in three dimensions, tiers from wafers (blue) to hyperscalers (red), sized by market capitalisation; arrows run from supplier to customer.
An interactive version with the filing behind every edge is on the [project page](https://vendraholdings.github.io/supply-chain-graph/).*

## Results

| Quantity | Value |
|---|---|
| Supplier-customer pairs (edge-years) | <<n_pairs>> (<<n_edge_rows>>) |
| Total spillover index, eleven tier indices, ten-day horizon (weekly data) | <<spill_total>> (<<spill_weekly>>) |
| Price shocks studied | <<n_shocks>> |
| Customer-momentum Sharpe ratio, net (gross); share of rewired-edge draws at or above it | <<sr_net>> (<<sr_gross>>); <<plc_p>> |
| Probability of backtest overfitting, deflated Sharpe ratio of the best specification | <<pbo>>, <<dsr>> |
| CAAR of direct suppliers over days 0 to 20 after a customer shock (n, adjusted t) | <<direct_supplier_caar>> (<<direct_supplier_n>>, <<direct_supplier_t>>) |
| Rejection rate of the 5% event-study test on random dates (direct suppliers) | <<direct_supplier_rej>> |

Equipment and packaging are the net transmitters of spillovers (<<tx1_v>> and <<tx2_v>> points); foundry and industrial gases are the net receivers (<<rx1_v>> and <<rx2_v>>).
No abnormal return of suppliers, customers or companies two links away is significant at the 5% level, with <<direct_supplier_n>>, <<direct_customer_n>> and <<two_hop_n>> events in the three groups; effects below one per cent are hard to detect.
Lead-lag correlations between tiers are mostly an effect of closing-time order between Asian and US markets. Every number above is generated by the code in this repository and quoted, with its method, in the [paper](paper/paper.pdf).

* **Live page:** <https://vendraholdings.github.io/supply-chain-graph/>
* **Paper:** [`paper/paper.pdf`](paper/paper.pdf)

